A deterministic version would look something like a revert of ef6a5a27c4d0cbc1f9b83e6e693740c4c66197ad.
```c#
var observedCancellation = new TaskCompletionSource(TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously);
```

```c#
var exception = await Assert.ThrowsAsync<TaskCanceledException>(() => task);
observedCancellation.SetResult();
```

```c#
await connection.SendResponseAsync(LoopbackServer.GetHttpResponseHeaders(contentLength: 100));
await Task.Yield();
cts.Cancel();
await observedCancellation.Task.WaitAsync(TestHelper.PassingTestTimeout);
```

But as written, this test isn't enforcing that the client actually honors the cancellation token before receiving the whole response.
The browser implementation seems to be struggling with this (I had to revert a similar change in #104384 due to test failures - [console logs](https://helixre107v0xdcypoyl9e7f.blob.core.windows.net/dotnet-runtime-refs-pull-104384-merge-e3831eca814647a093/WasmTestOnFirefox-MT-System.Net.Http.Functional.Tests/1/console.8f17870c.log?helixlogtype=result#:~:text=%5B21%3A14%3A37%5D%20info%3A%20System.AggregateException%20%3A%20One%20or%20more%20errors%20occurred.%20(TypeError%3A%20NetworkError%20when%20attempting%20to%20fetch%20resource.)%20(The%20operation%20has%20timed%20out.))). cc: @pavelsavara 

Given that this test is a copy-paste of existing test logic we have in this file, I'd keep it as-is in this PR.
